  "conclusions": "BMA posterior-weighted combination tested vs single-best (GJR-t) and equal-weight ensemble on SPY/GLD/0050.TW 2020-2026 OOS. Per-asset summary: SPY: n_common=1518, BMA=-8.18639, GJR-t=-8.13579 (BMA-GJR=-0.05060, Harvey t=-3.17); Equal=-8.16675 (BMA-Eq=-0.01964, Harvey t=-1.37) | GLD: n_common=1581, BMA=-8.18117, GJR-t=-8.09042 (BMA-GJR=-0.09075, Harvey t=-3.38); Equal=-8.13711 (BMA-Eq=-0.04406, Harvey t=-2.69) | 0050.TW: n_common=1524, BMA=-7.67905, GJR-t=-7.68246 (BMA-GJR=+0.00342, Harvey t=+0.98); Equal=-7.69398 (BMA-Eq=+0.01493, Harvey t=+1.68). H1 verdict: PARTIAL; H2: FAIL; H3 (regime weight shift): FAIL. All headline QLIKE figures are computed on the common valid days shared by BMA / Equal / GJR-t (n_common per asset above), the same sample the Harvey t is built from; per-series own-sample means are kept separately under qlike_own_sample. Research-honesty: null results reported as-is; the purpose of BMA is principled posterior weighting, not guaranteed QLIKE win. Posterior concentration is not hypothetical here \u2014 on every asset the terminal posterior puts ~1.0 on a single model, so BMA is in practice that model's forecast and the gap vs single-best is a statement about which model the posterior picked, not about combination gains. Invalid-day exclusion is absorbing (see posterior_semantics): a model dropped after a non-converged refit cannot regain weight.",
  "runtime_seconds": 369.6,
  "quick_mode": false
